<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Thomas Scott (Orangeman)</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">c. 1842–<a href="/years/1870">1870</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/19th-century">19th century</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Thomas Scott was an Irish-born Protestant labourer and member of the Orange Order who opposed <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/louis-riel">Louis Riel</a>'s provisional government at Red River. Captured during an armed counter-movement, he was tried by a <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/metis">Métis</a> tribunal and executed by firing squad on 4 March 1870. His death enraged Protestant <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ontario">Ontario</a> and turned Riel into a hunted man.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/thomas-scott-orangeman.jpg" alt="Thomas Scott (Orangeman)" width="284" height="255" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:OrangemanThomasScott.gif" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Early life and background</h2><p>Little is known of Scott's early years. He was born in 1842 in the Clandeboye area of County Down, was raised a Presbyterian and emigrated to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ontario">Ontario</a> in 1863. At Red River he worked as a labourer on the Dawson Road, which was to link the settlement with Lake Superior, until a strike in 1869 got him dismissed and convicted of aggravated assault. In Winnipeg he met John Christian Schultz, joined the Canadian Party and backed the annexation of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/red-river-colony">Red River Settlement</a> to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/canada">Canada</a>.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Career</h2><p>In December 1869 Riel's men arrested him at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/upper-fort-garry">Upper Fort Garry</a> with the party of 45 who had fortified Schultz's store against the Métis National Committee. He escaped in January 1870, as did Schultz and Charles Mair. In February he was among several hundred armed volunteers who gathered at Kildonan Presbyterian Church to free the remaining prisoners; Riel released them and the volunteers dispersed, but Scott's group passed too close to Fort Garry on the way to Portage la Prairie and he was taken again. In captivity he was ill, abusive and refused to recognise his captors' authority, and after a fight with guards he was moved to a stronger cell.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Death and legacy</h2><p>A small <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/metis">Métis</a> court, sitting in private without outside observers, found him guilty of defying the provisional government, fighting the guards and slandering Riel; an eyewitness, Nolin, later testified that Scott was absent when the charges and evidence were heard. Others were sentenced with him but only he was shot. Accounts of his last moments disagree, and two witnesses, John Bruce and Major George Robinson, claimed he survived the volley in a makeshift coffin. His body was never found.</p><p>The English-speaking, Protestant population of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ontario">Ontario</a> hailed him as a martyr, and the Toronto Globe wrote that he had been murdered by enemies of the Queen, country and religion. The historian Lyle Dick has said his death gave expansionists a rallying symbol that sped the dispatch of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/red-river-expedition-1870">Wolseley Expedition</a>, which drove Riel from the settlement. For a century English-speaking historians tended to depict him as an innocent victim and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/french-canadians">French Canadians</a> to stress his behaviour; J. M. Bumsted finds agreement only that the execution was a political mistake by Riel and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/metis">Métis</a>.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Thomas Scott (Orangeman)” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>It held prisoners from the Canadian party, and in March 1870 executed one of them, <b>Thomas Scott</b>, an act that inflamed Protestant Ontario.</blockquote><a href="/state/provisional-government-of-assiniboia">Provisional Government of Assiniboia</a></li></ul></section>
<section class="enc-where enc-born"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Born and died</h2><p class="enc-born-p">Died in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/upper-fort-garry">Fort Garry</a>, in what is now <a href="/countries/canada">Canada</a>. <span class="enc-born-src">Places as Wikidata records them.</span></p><figure class="st-locator enc-loc"><a href="/atlas?at=49.89,-97.14&amp;year=1860" aria-label="Fort Garry on the atlas"><svg class="map map-globe map-loc" viewBox="0 0 10000 10000" role="img" aria-label="Globe: the place of death of Thomas Scott (Orangeman)"><image href="/maps/globes/g270n60.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="10000"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M4608 5839h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M4608 5839h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks the place of death. <a href="/atlas?at=49.89,-97.14&amp;year=1860">What was here in 1860? &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></section>
